WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.120 --> 00:00:04.780
CHARLES FRIED: OK, so we've got this idea of bargain.

00:00:04.780 --> 00:00:08.340
And the bargain doesn't have to be a good bargain.

00:00:08.340 --> 00:00:11.290
It just has to be a real bargain.

00:00:11.290 --> 00:00:13.530
Are you ready for a curve ball?

00:00:13.530 --> 00:00:19.100
This is a famous case from the beginning of the 20th century.

00:00:19.100 --> 00:00:26.420
The business of canning Alaska's plentiful salmon was new and promising.

00:00:26.420 --> 00:00:30.000
Companies would set up factories and docks along

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:35.520
the Alaska panhandle which, at that time-- and still today in some parts--

00:00:35.520 --> 00:00:39.120
could only be reached by ship.

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:42.640
Did you know that Juneau, Alaska, which is in the panhandle,

00:00:42.640 --> 00:00:45.760
cannot be reached by car?

00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:49.770
You either have to get there by ship or by airplane, even today.

00:00:49.770 --> 00:00:55.080
The season was very short-- just the summer months, during which time

00:00:55.080 --> 00:00:59.390
all the fish would have to be caught, processed, canned,

00:00:59.390 --> 00:01:05.180
and the cans brought out by ship, usually to Seattle or San Francisco.

00:01:05.180 --> 00:01:10.000
The canneries would sign up crews in Seattle or San Francisco

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:16.910
to do the fishing and work the machinery for a fixed rate for the season, paid

00:01:16.910 --> 00:01:19.430
on return to the city.

00:01:19.430 --> 00:01:24.130
The workers in this case were signed on in San Francisco

00:01:24.130 --> 00:01:26.770
for $50 for the season.

00:01:26.770 --> 00:01:30.030
I told you this was at the beginning of the 20th century.

00:01:30.030 --> 00:01:34.130
And they got to Alaska, to the Alaska station,

00:01:34.130 --> 00:01:38.750
and there they refused to work unless the foreman agreed

00:01:38.750 --> 00:01:42.610
to pay them $100 for the season.

00:01:42.610 --> 00:01:45.860
It was too late to get replacements.

00:01:45.860 --> 00:01:49.130
And because he didn't want to lose the whole season,

00:01:49.130 --> 00:01:56.060
the foreman agreed and signed new contracts with each crew member.

00:01:56.060 --> 00:02:00.180
At the end of the season, they get back in San Francisco,

00:02:00.180 --> 00:02:07.590
and the company refused to honor the second contract, and paid only $50.

00:02:07.590 --> 00:02:10.990
The workers sued for the difference.

00:02:10.990 --> 00:02:12.800
What do you think happened?

00:02:12.800 --> 00:02:15.000
There was a bargain, wasn't there?

00:02:15.000 --> 00:02:17.870
It may not have been a good bargain.

00:02:17.870 --> 00:02:20.420
But it was a bargain.

